    {
      "id": "1211c401-8db1-415f-b35f-ece3401abf94",
      "name": "Why MCP is awesome: one protocol, every tool",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is awesome: one protocol, every tool",
      "docTypeId": "e04b5e99-4f16-45e1-bb9e-3e00da70f796",
      "templateId": "cc8b84b2-2207-4e85-93c3-9e11eb8876a0",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "Before MCP, every integration was a bespoke cable. Now there is one plug both sides speak — and the content stack is no exception."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "9d0abbb7-f3ea-40af-8942-d1387c775f44",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "020534d5-456b-4188-9304-96cdade4f6bf",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "1eb9f248-640e-4114-8423-7c8f4f9b6190",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "86004d4f-1443-462f-a688-24841de05a8e",
                "key": "9d0abbb7-f3ea-40af-8942-d1387c775f44",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "credit",
                    "value": "Photo: Lucian Alexe / Unsplash"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>Every integration used to be a bespoke cable: a custom connector for the CMS, another for the CRM, another for analytics. Each one had to be designed, built, and maintained on both ends. MCP replaces that tangle with a single, open protocol — one plug that both sides already speak.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "0b7193a1-6f2f-4a2a-9274-8f4e1e2ab001",
                        "mediaKey": "61aa533d-fab8-4189-8249-89a868d9784b",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "bf41e086-ac6a-44cb-a0a9-aaa276600da7",
                "key": "020534d5-456b-4188-9304-96cdade4f6bf",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<h2>The M×N problem</h2><p>Think of the old state of affairs as an M×N problem: M applications that each want to reach N tools and data sources. Without a shared protocol, the only answer was custom integrations — M×N bespoke cables, each one its own little project with its own auth quirks, its own data shapes, its own failure modes. It is the same reason USB replaced a drawer full of proprietary chargers.</p><p>MCP collapses that to M+N: an application implements the protocol once, a tool server implements it once, and they can talk. New tool? Plug it in. New client? It already knows how to listen.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "bf41e086-ac6a-44cb-a0a9-aaa276600da7",
                "key": "1eb9f248-640e-4114-8423-7c8f4f9b6190",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<h2>Why it matters for content</h2><p>For a CMS, this is quietly a big deal. An MCP server exposes the content model — document types, media, publishing — as tools and resources, so anything from an editor's assistant to a build pipeline can work with the same well-defined surface. The value is not that the protocol is clever; it is that it is <em>boring</em>: standard, open, and already implemented everywhere. That is exactly what a protocol should be.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "9d0abbb7-f3ea-40af-8942-d1387c775f44",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "020534d5-456b-4188-9304-96cdade4f6bf",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "1eb9f248-640e-4114-8423-7c8f4f9b6190",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/why-mcp-is-awesome-one-protocol-every-tool/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    },
    {
      "id": "e93b20b6-a89e-44ec-9f37-0449764109cf",
      "name": "Why MCP is awesome: agents that can actually help",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is awesome: agents that can actually help",
      "docTypeId": "e04b5e99-4f16-45e1-bb9e-3e00da70f796",
      "templateId": "cc8b84b2-2207-4e85-93c3-9e11eb8876a0",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "An AI assistant is only as useful as the things it can reach. MCP gives agents a safe, standard way to reach your CMS — this very post is proof."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.BlockGrid",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "blocks",
          "value": {
            "layout": {
              "Umbraco.BlockGrid": [
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "395c3d87-0a63-42fc-87bc-28d4ee9a65b7",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "17bffdea-618a-4ba1-a6de-b8e880ad95a8",
                  "settingsKey": null
                },
                {
                  "$type": "BlockGridLayoutItem",
                  "columnSpan": 12,
                  "rowSpan": 1,
                  "areas": [],
                  "contentKey": "03941582-4435-4189-941e-f9222a6e8d3e",
                  "settingsKey": null
                }
              ]
            },
            "contentData": [
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "bf41e086-ac6a-44cb-a0a9-aaa276600da7",
                "key": "395c3d87-0a63-42fc-87bc-28d4ee9a65b7",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>An AI assistant is only as useful as the things it can actually reach. A brilliant writing partner that cannot see the content tree, the media library or the publish button is a partner locked out of the building. MCP changes that: it gives agents a standard, permissioned way to work with real systems — including a CMS like Umbraco.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "86004d4f-1443-462f-a688-24841de05a8e",
                "key": "17bffdea-618a-4ba1-a6de-b8e880ad95a8",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "credit",
                    "value": "Photo: Edge2Edge Media / Unsplash"
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "image",
                    "value": [
                      {
                        "key": "b00cc4e2-3a2a-4a71-8a55-11e2c993ab02",
                        "mediaKey": "5c3b91a3-bd9b-46ec-930e-d9a6df9ae099",
                        "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
                        "crops": [],
                        "focalPoint": null
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<p>With an MCP server, the assistant does not have to guess what an <em>article</em> is or how a <em>hero</em> is put together. The content model itself tells it: here are the types, here are the fields, here is what publishes. The agent works the way an editor works — creating drafts, filling in blocks, and asking before anything goes live.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "contentTypeKey": "bf41e086-ac6a-44cb-a0a9-aaa276600da7",
                "key": "03941582-4435-4189-941e-f9222a6e8d3e",
                "values": [
                  {
                    "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
                    "culture": null,
                    "segment": null,
                    "alias": "text",
                    "value": {
                      "markup": "<h2>This post is the demo</h2><p>There is a neat bit of recursion here: this page, the hero on the front page, and the images in both posts were all created through Umbraco's MCP server. No bespoke script, no hand-poked database — just a protocol conversation. The assistant proposed the schema, the user confirmed each action, and the result is a site that was modeled and filled in minutes.</p><p>That is the promise of MCP: not magic, just a common language. And once everything speaks it, the interesting question stops being <em>can</em> the agent reach the CMS, and becomes <em>what should we ask it to do next</em>.</p>",
                      "blocks": {
                        "layout": {},
                        "contentData": [],
                        "settingsData": [],
                        "expose": []
                      }
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ],
            "settingsData": [],
            "expose": [
              {
                "contentKey": "395c3d87-0a63-42fc-87bc-28d4ee9a65b7",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "17bffdea-618a-4ba1-a6de-b8e880ad95a8",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              },
              {
                "contentKey": "03941582-4435-4189-941e-f9222a6e8d3e",
                "culture": null,
                "segment": null
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/why-mcp-is-awesome-agents-that-can-actually-help/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    }
